(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-08"] = {"chem-12-08-m01":{"idea":"A true transition element needs a partly filled d subshell, in the atom or in a common ion.","steps":["The definition looks at the d subshell.","The d subshell must be incomplete in the atom or in at least one common ion.","Zn, Cd and Hg have d¹⁰ in the atom and in the +2 ion.","So they are d-block elements but not transition elements."],"others":"A partly filled s subshell is not the test. A full d subshell in the atom and all ions would exclude the metals, not define them. Having one outer s electron is true of only a few elements.","trap":"d-block and transition are not the same thing.","answer":"an incompletely filled d subshell in its atoms or in any of its common ions"},"chem-12-08-m02":{"idea":"The reason is the full d¹⁰ configuration, not the physical state.","steps":["Zn, Cd and Hg have the configuration d¹⁰ s² in the atom.","Their common +2 ions are d¹⁰ too.","A full d subshell gives no colour, no paramagnetism and no variable oxidation states.","So they are not counted as transition elements."],"others":"Only mercury is a liquid, and a physical state is not a reason anyway. These atoms do have d electrons, ten of them. They form many compounds.","trap":"Full d¹⁰ means no typical transition properties.","answer":"their atoms and their common +2 ions have a full d¹⁰ configuration"},"chem-12-08-m03":{"idea":"Half-filled and full d subshells are extra stable.","steps":["Cr would be 3d⁴ 4s² by simple filling.","One 4s electron moves into 3d to give the stable half-filled 3d⁵ 4s¹.","Cu would be 3d⁹ 4s² by simple filling.","One 4s electron moves in to give the full 3d¹⁰ 4s¹."],"others":"The pairs that show 3d⁴ 4s² for Cr or 3d⁹ 4s² for Cu follow simple filling and miss the shift. The pair with 3d⁵ 4s¹ for Cr and 3d⁹ 4s² for Cu is correct for Cr only.","trap":"Cr and Cu are the two exceptions in the first row.","answer":"[Ar]3d⁵ 4s¹ and [Ar]3d¹⁰ 4s¹"},"chem-12-08-m04":{"idea":"Atoms lose the outer shell electrons first.","steps":["In the atom, 4s is the outer shell, even though it fills before 3d.","When ions form, the 4s electrons leave first.","Fe has 3d⁶ 4s². Fe²⁺ loses both 4s electrons.","So Fe²⁺ is [Ar]3d⁶."],"others":"3p electrons are in an inner shell and are not removed in normal ion formation. Losing 3d first would give a wrong configuration. Equal loss from both has no basis.","trap":"Fill 4s first, remove 4s first.","answer":"the 4s orbital, before the 3d orbitals"},"chem-12-08-m05":{"idea":"Many oxidation states arise because two sets of electrons are close in energy.","steps":["The (n−1)d and ns orbitals have nearly the same energy.","After the ns electrons are lost (+2), d electrons can also be used.","So the metal can lose different numbers of electrons.","This gives states that differ by one unit, such as Mn from +2 to +7."],"others":"They do have empty d orbitals, so that choice is wrong. d electrons are not held very strongly, since they take part in bonding. The outer shell does not have a very large number of electrons.","trap":"Close energy of ns and (n−1)d is the key phrase.","answer":"the (n−1)d and ns electrons have nearly the same energy, so both can take part in bonding"},"chem-12-08-m06":{"idea":"The highest state needs all valence electrons to take part.","steps":["Mn has configuration 3d⁵ 4s², seven electrons outside the argon core.","It can use all seven in MnO₄⁻, which gives +7.","Cr reaches only +6, and Cu reaches only +2 or +3.","Fe shows up to +6 in rare ions, so it is lower than +7."],"others":"Chromium in CrO₄²⁻ is +6. Copper in CuO is +2. Iron in FeO₄²⁻ is +6. All three are below +7.","trap":"Mn has the highest state in the 3d series: +7.","answer":"manganese, in MnO₄⁻"},"chem-12-08-m07":{"idea":"Redox behaviour follows stability of the product ion.","steps":["Cr³⁺ is d³ and is stable. Mn²⁺ is d⁵, which is half-filled and stable.","Cr²⁺ is d⁴, so it readily loses one electron to become Cr³⁺. It is a strong reducing agent.","Mn³⁺ is d⁴, so it readily gains one electron to become Mn²⁺. It is a strong oxidising agent."],"others":"Cr²⁺ and Mn³⁺ are the unstable ions, so they are not the most stable. Atomic size is not the reason. Cr³⁺ is d³ and Mn²⁺ is d⁵, so neither has an empty d subshell.","trap":"Stable products drive the reaction.","answer":"Cr³⁺ (d³) and Mn²⁺ (d⁵) are especially stable configurations"},"chem-12-08-m08":{"idea":"Colour needs a d–d transition.","steps":["A d–d transition moves an electron from one d orbital to a higher one by absorbing visible light.","Sc³⁺ is d⁰, so there is no electron to move.","Zn²⁺ is d¹⁰, so there is no empty d orbital to move into.","So neither can show d–d colour."],"others":"Half-filled d orbitals can still allow transitions. High ionisation energy has no link to colour. These ions do have electrons in other orbitals, only the d subshell is empty or full.","trap":"d⁰ and d¹⁰ ions are colourless.","answer":"a d⁰ and a d¹⁰ configuration, with no possible d–d transition"},"chem-12-08-m09":{"idea":"Magnetic moment grows with the number of unpaired electrons.","steps":["Ti³⁺ is d¹ with 1 unpaired electron.","Mn²⁺ is d⁵ with 5 unpaired electrons.","Cu⁺ and Zn²⁺ are d¹⁰ with none.","Mn²⁺ has the most, so the highest moment, √35 = 5.92 BM."],"others":"Zn²⁺ has no unpaired electrons, not two. Ti³⁺ has one, not three. Cu⁺ has none, not one.","trap":"Write the d count first, then count unpaired electrons.","answer":"Mn²⁺, which has 5 unpaired electrons"},"chem-12-08-m10":{"idea":"A good catalyst can form short-lived links with the reactants.","steps":["Transition metals show variable oxidation states.","They can form intermediate compounds with reactants.","Vacant d orbitals also bind reactants on the surface.","This gives a path with a lower activation energy."],"others":"A high melting point does not make a catalyst. Electrical conduction is not the reason. A catalyst is not inert, since it takes part in the mechanism and is regained at the end.","trap":"Variable oxidation state is the usual reason.","answer":"show variable oxidation states and can form intermediate compounds"},"chem-12-08-m11":{"idea":"Interstitial compounds are formed when small atoms sit in gaps.","steps":["Metal atoms in a lattice leave small empty spaces, called voids.","Small atoms such as H, C or N fit in these voids.","Examples are TiC and Fe₃H.","They have no fixed formula and the metal lattice stays."],"others":"Two similar-size metals mixing is a solid solution or alloy. Replacing atoms in a non-metal lattice is not how these form. A reaction with acid is a salt-forming reaction.","trap":"Interstitial means in the gaps.","answer":"small atoms such as H, C or N occupy the voids of the metal lattice"},"chem-12-08-m12":{"idea":"Know the common alloys.","steps":["Brass is made of copper and zinc.","Usually it holds 20 to 40 percent zinc.","Bronze is copper and tin.","Steel is iron and carbon."],"others":"Iron and carbon makes steel. Copper and tin makes bronze. Copper and nickel only is cupronickel, not brass.","trap":"Brass = Cu + Zn. Bronze = Cu + Sn.","answer":"copper and zinc"},"chem-12-08-m13":{"idea":"Strong metallic bonding gives high melting points.","steps":["In transition metals both the ns and the (n−1)d electrons take part in bonding.","More shared electrons mean a stronger metallic bond.","Stronger bonds need more heat to break.","So the melting points are high."],"others":"Metals do not have ionic bonds between layers. Using only the ns electrons would give weaker bonds. Weak van der Waals forces would give low melting points.","trap":"Zn, Cd and Hg have full d shells, so they melt low.","answer":"both the ns and the (n−1)d electrons take part in strong metallic bonding"},"chem-12-08-m14":{"idea":"Two effects compete across the series: nuclear charge and shielding.","steps":["Going from Sc to Zn, nuclear charge rises.","Extra electrons go into the inner 3d subshell, which shields the outer electrons.","At first the pull wins and radii fall.","In the middle the two effects nearly cancel, so radii stay nearly constant."],"others":"Radii do not rise steadily. They are not exactly constant. They do not fall steadily to zinc, because the 3d electrons shield the nucleus in the later part.","trap":"Fall, then flat.","answer":"fall at first and then stay nearly constant, as d electrons shield the nucleus"},"chem-12-08-m15":{"idea":"The lanthanoid contraction is a decrease in size along the series.","steps":["Across La to Lu, the 4f subshell fills.","4f electrons shield the nuclear charge poorly.","So the effective nuclear charge grows.","The atoms and ions get smaller, from La³⁺ (106 pm) to Lu³⁺ (85 pm)."],"others":"Oxidation state does not fall along the series. Ionic size does not rise. The term has nothing to do with heating.","trap":"Contraction = decrease in size with atomic number.","answer":"steady decrease in the size of the lanthanoid atoms and ions with increasing atomic number"},"chem-12-08-m16":{"idea":"The lanthanoid contraction cancels the usual size increase between the 4d and 5d series.","steps":["Normally an atom is bigger when the next shell starts.","The lanthanoids come between Zr (4d) and Hf (5d).","Their contraction nearly cancels the increase.","So Zr (160 pm) and Hf (159 pm) are almost the same size and similar in properties."],"others":"Zr and Hf do not have very different radii. The 3d elements are smaller than the 4d elements, not larger. Oxidation states of the lanthanoids are not the effect in question.","trap":"Zr and Hf are twins because of the contraction.","answer":"zirconium and hafnium have almost the same atomic radius and very similar properties"},"chem-12-08-m17":{"idea":"The dichromate–chromate equilibrium depends on pH.","steps":["The equilibrium is Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 2OH⁻ ⇌ 2CrO₄²⁻ + H₂O.","Adding alkali supplies OH⁻, so the equilibrium moves to the right.","Chromate ions, CrO₄²⁻, are yellow.","So the orange solution turns yellow. Acid reverses this."],"others":"Purple would mean permanganate, which has Mn, not Cr. Green is the colour of Cr³⁺, which needs a reducing agent. Chromium is not precipitated here.","trap":"Alkali: yellow. Acid: orange.","answer":"yellow, as chromate ions form"},"chem-12-08-m18":{"idea":"In acid, dichromate is reduced from +6 to +3.","steps":["The half reaction is Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O.","Each Cr goes from +6 to +3.","Cr³⁺ is green.","So the orange solution turns green."],"others":"Cr₂O₃ is a solid, not the product in acid. CrO₄²⁻ is the same +6 state, so it is not a reduction. Cr²⁺ would need a much stronger reducing agent.","trap":"Orange to green is the dichromate test for reducing agents.","answer":"Cr³⁺, which is green"},"chem-12-08-m19":{"idea":"In acid, permanganate is reduced from +7 to +2.","steps":["The half reaction is MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O.","Mn goes from +7 to +2.","Mn²⁺ is nearly colourless.","So the purple colour disappears."],"others":"Mn³⁺ is not the usual product. MnO₄²⁻ forms in strongly alkaline solution. MnO₂ forms in neutral or weakly alkaline solution.","trap":"Acid gives Mn²⁺. Neutral gives MnO₂.","answer":"Mn²⁺, which is nearly colourless"},"chem-12-08-m20":{"idea":"The product of permanganate depends on the medium.","steps":["In neutral or weakly alkaline solution: MnO₄⁻ + 2H₂O + 3e⁻ → MnO₂ + 4OH⁻.","Mn goes from +7 to +4.","MnO₂ is a brown precipitate.","So the answer is MnO₂."],"others":"MnO₄²⁻ forms in strongly alkaline solution. Mn metal needs a far stronger reductant. Mn²⁺ forms in acid.","trap":"Acid: Mn²⁺. Neutral: MnO₂. Strong alkali: MnO₄²⁻.","answer":"MnO₂, a brown precipitate"},"chem-12-08-m21":{"idea":"Making permanganate has two stages.","steps":["MnO₂ is fused with KOH in air: 2MnO₂ + 4KOH + O₂ → 2K₂MnO₄ + 2H₂O.","This gives green potassium manganate, with Mn at +6.","Then it is oxidised, by electrolysis or by chlorine or ozone.","That gives purple permanganate, with Mn at +7."],"others":"MnO₂ does change when fused with KOH in air. Permanganate does not form directly in one step. Manganese(II) sulphate is not formed in this reaction.","trap":"Manganate is green, +6. Permanganate is purple, +7.","answer":"green potassium manganate, K₂MnO₄, later oxidised to permanganate"},"chem-12-08-m22":{"idea":"All lanthanoids have +3 as the common state.","steps":["All the lanthanoids form Ln³⁺ ions.","They lose the two 6s electrons and one more electron.","Ce can show +4, and Eu can show +2.","These are exceptions linked to f⁰ and f⁷ stability."],"others":"+5 and +4 are rare for lanthanoids. +2 is shown mainly by Eu. They are not the common state.","trap":"Lanthanoids: +3. Actinoids: many states.","answer":"+3"},"chem-12-08-m23":{"idea":"All actinoids share one property.","steps":["Every actinoid has an unstable nucleus.","So all of them are radioactive.","Only thorium and uranium are found in appreciable amounts in nature.","Elements beyond uranium are made artificially."],"others":"Actinoids show many oxidation states, not only +3. They are not stable. Most are not found in large quantities in nature.","trap":"Actinoids: radioactive, always.","answer":"they are radioactive"},"chem-12-08-m24":{"idea":"More oxidation states need more electrons that can take part in bonding.","steps":["In actinoids, the 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals have close energies.","So 5f electrons can also take part in bonding.","This gives several states, for example U shows +3, +4, +5 and +6.","In lanthanoids the 4f electrons are deep inside, so they are mostly +3."],"others":"Actinoids are not much smaller than lanthanoids. They do have f electrons. The 5f and 7s orbitals are close in energy, not far apart.","trap":"Close energies, so more states.","answer":"the 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals are close in energy, so more electrons can bond"},"chem-12-08-m25":{"idea":"A lanthanoid alloy is used for lighter flints.","steps":["Mischmetall is an alloy of lanthanoids, about 95% lanthanoids and 5% iron.","It gives sparks when struck.","So it is used in lighter flints.","It is also added to steel to remove oxygen and sulphur."],"others":"Brass has no lanthanoids and is not used for flints. Stainless steel with chromium has no lanthanoids. Solder is lead and tin and has no lanthanoids.","trap":"Mischmetall means mixed metals.","answer":"mischmetall (about 95% lanthanoids and 5% iron) in lighter flints"},"chem-12-08-m26":{"idea":"Limestone is a flux. It removes impurities.","steps":["Heat breaks limestone: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.","CaO reacts with silica, SiO₂, in the ore.","CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃, which is the slag.","The slag floats on molten iron and is removed."],"others":"Iron is not dissolved by limestone. Limestone does not raise the oxygen content. Iron oxide is reduced by carbon monoxide from coke, not by limestone.","trap":"Coke reduces. Limestone removes silica.","answer":"remove silica as slag, which is calcium silicate"},"chem-12-08-n01":{"idea":"Spin-only magnetic moment depends on the unpaired electrons, n.","steps":["μ = √(n(n + 2)) Bohr magnetons (BM).","Mn²⁺ has n = 5.","μ = √(5 × 7) = √35.","μ = 5.92 BM."],"trap":"Check: √36 = 6, so √35 must be a little below 6.","answer":"5.92 BM"},"chem-12-08-n02":{"idea":"Spin-only magnetic moment depends on the unpaired electrons, n.","steps":["μ = √(n(n + 2)) BM.","Cu²⁺ is d⁹, so n = 1.","μ = √(1 × 3) = √3.","μ = 1.73 BM."],"trap":"Check: √3 is about 1.73.","answer":"1.73 BM"},"chem-12-08-n03":{"idea":"Remove the 4s electrons first, then the 3d.","steps":["Fe is [Ar]3d⁶ 4s².","For Fe³⁺, three electrons leave.","The two 4s electrons go first, then one 3d electron.","3d⁶ becomes 3d⁵, so there are 5 electrons."],"trap":"Check: d⁵ is half-filled, which explains why Fe³⁺ is so stable.","answer":"5"},"chem-12-08-n04":{"idea":"Equivalent mass is molar mass divided by the electrons gained per formula unit.","steps":["In acid, Mn goes from +7 to +2.","So each MnO₄⁻ gains 5 electrons.","Equivalent mass = 158/5.","Equivalent mass = 31.6 g/equiv."],"trap":"Check: n-factor is 5, so the equivalent mass is one fifth of the molar mass.","answer":"31.6 g/equiv"},"chem-12-08-n05":{"idea":"Use the mole ratio of MnO₄⁻ to Fe²⁺, which is 1 : 5.","steps":["Moles of KMnO₄ = 0.02 mol/L × 0.020 L = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol.","Each MnO₄⁻ oxidises 5 Fe²⁺.","Moles of Fe²⁺ = 5 × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol.","That is 2.00 mmol."],"trap":"Check: 20 mL × 0.02 M = 0.4 mmol of KMnO₄, and 0.4 × 5 = 2.0 mmol.","answer":"2.00 mmol"},"chem-12-08-n06":{"idea":"Equivalent mass is molar mass divided by the electrons gained per formula unit.","steps":["Each Cr goes from +6 to +3, gaining 3 electrons.","There are 2 Cr atoms, so 6 electrons per formula unit.","Equivalent mass = 294/6.","Equivalent mass = 49.0 g/equiv."],"trap":"Check: the n-factor is 6, since there are two Cr atoms.","answer":"49.0 g/equiv"},"chem-12-08-n07":{"idea":"Use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","steps":["The equation shows 2MnO₄⁻ react with 5C₂O₄²⁻.","So the ratio of MnO₄⁻ to oxalate is 2 : 5.","Moles of KMnO₄ = 0.5 × 2/5.","Moles of KMnO₄ = 0.20 mol."],"trap":"Check: the electrons match, since 0.2 × 5 = 1.0 and 0.5 × 2 = 1.0.","answer":"0.20 mol"},"chem-12-08-n08":{"idea":"Oxidation numbers in a neutral compound add to zero.","steps":["K is +1 and O is −2.","2(+1) + 2x + 7(−2) = 0.","2 + 2x − 14 = 0.","2x = 12, so x = +6."],"trap":"Check: K₂Cr₂O₇ has Cr at +6, just like chromate.","answer":"+6"},"chem-12-08-n09":{"idea":"Percentage by mass is the part's mass divided by the total, times 100.","steps":["Mass of 2 Fe = 2 × 55.85 = 111.7.","Mass of 3 O = 3 × 16 = 48.","Molar mass of Fe₂O₃ = 159.7 g/mol.","Percentage of Fe = 111.7/159.7 × 100 = 69.94%."],"trap":"Check: oxygen is 48/159.7 = 30.06%, and 100 − 30.06 = 69.94.","answer":"69.94%"},"chem-12-08-n10":{"idea":"Remove the outer electrons first. The 4f subshell is inside.","steps":["Gd is [Xe]4f⁷ 5d¹ 6s².","For Gd³⁺, three electrons are lost.","The 5d electron and the two 6s electrons leave.","4f⁷ is left, so there are 7 electrons."],"trap":"Check: 4f⁷ is half-filled and stable.","answer":"7"}};
